  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 107 AM MDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nThe sprawling high will continue to overlay the southern states \ninto Wednesday, with pressure rises modeled over NM (500 mb \nheights reaching near 596 decameters over western portions of the \nstate). This would yield a degree or two of warming, but a \nbackdoor front entering northeastern zones will likely reduce \nreadings by a couple to a few degrees, particularly in Colfax and \nUnion counties. Otherwise, moisture will be readily available over\nmost of the forecast area except the northwestern quadrant of the\nstate where some drier air will have wrapped in. This will again \nleave out those northwestern zones and southeastern zones for any \nstorm potential Wednesday while the areas along and east of the \nSangre de Cristos see an uptick due to upslope and \nsurface/moisture convergence. \n\nHigh pressure looks to stay over or to the southwest of NM into \nThursday and Friday. Drier westerly flow will will begin to become \nmore invasive Thursday (mainly over northwestern to north central \nzones) and more-so into Friday (over much of northern and central \nNM). With PWATs falling to 0.6 to 0.7 inch in many of these drier \nareas, storms will reduce in number. This will send temperatures \nclimbing 5 to almost 15 degrees above normal.\n\nInto the upcoming weekend, the pattern will potentially start to \nturn a bit more dynamic with the high shifting east of NM while \nanother deep upper low moves into the Pacific Northwest states. \nThis would allow subtropical moisture to be squeezed northward \ninto NM, between the upper level features, and POPs increase again\nwith scattered coverage favoring the western and central high \nterrain. Temperatures would ease down closer to, but still \nslightly above, normal.",
